analysisWe need to produce an assert statement corresponding to the comment.

We have method removeFirstOccurrence(Object o) which returns boolean result of remove(o). The comment: "// @@@ If the list initially contains the specified element, then the result of the removal operation is true."

We need to express: if the list initially contains the element (i.e., before operation), then the returned result is true. Using \old to refer to pre-state. The method returns boolean, we refer to \result as return value. So specification: \old(this.contains(o)) => \result == true. In boolean context, we can just say \old(this.contains(o)) => \result. Since \result is boolean, implication to true is same as \old(this.contains(o)) => \result. Usually we write \old(this.contains(o)) => \result == true, but can just be \old(this.contains(o)) => \result.

Thus final assert: assert \old(this.contains(o)) => \result;.

